1
00:00:00,366 --> 00:00:03,433
At 7 pm, a dozen people are lined up outside the bubble tea shop.

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,300
The owner grits his teeth and calls in another worker.

3
00:00:06,566 --> 00:00:09,633
Standing in line, you think: now it should go faster.

4
00:00:09,766 --> 00:00:15,633
Ten minutes later the line hasn't moved.
Four workers are jammed behind the counter, and none of them are idle.

5
00:00:15,766 --> 00:00:20,100
Even the owner is puzzled — one more person, but barely any faster.

6
00:00:20,266 --> 00:00:24,766
Honestly, my first instinct used to be: the new hire just isn't skilled yet.

7
00:00:24,866 --> 00:00:30,733
But once I did the math, I found the problem wasn't the person — it was the machine behind them.

8
00:00:31,066 --> 00:00:33,766
This is Pixel Breath's 100 Principles of Economics.

9
00:00:33,766 --> 00:00:36,733
Principle #6: the Law of Diminishing Marginal Returns.

10
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:40,133
No definitions to memorize — just a real-life tally to make it click.

11
00:00:41,366 --> 00:00:44,333
Let's set some numbers for this shop to make the math easy.

12
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:46,233
One cup of bubble tea costs 15 yuan.

13
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:53,233
With just one worker behind the counter, they handle orders, ingredients, shaking, sealing, and packing — all alone.

14
00:00:53,433 --> 00:00:55,933
In an hour, they turn out about 30 cups.

15
00:00:56,266 --> 00:01:02,266
A second worker joins. They split the work: one takes orders and mixes, the other seals and packs.

16
00:01:02,566 --> 00:01:08,033
The steps one person used to run back and forth for are now split in two. Output hits 60 cups an hour.

17
00:01:08,266 --> 00:01:12,833
Exactly double. More hands, more output — no problem here.

18
00:01:12,966 --> 00:01:17,466
A third worker joins, dedicated to restocking ice and cups, prepping ingredients ahead of time.

19
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:19,433
Output climbs to 80 cups an hour.

20
00:01:19,633 --> 00:01:25,000
The owner sees each new hire adds 20 cups — so surely one more gets them to 100?

21
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:26,466
A fourth worker joins.

22
00:01:26,466 --> 00:01:28,066
85 cups an hour.

23
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:29,133
Just 5 more.

24
00:01:29,366 --> 00:01:35,500
Add a fifth, and it might reach 87 — or the counter gets so crowded it does worse than with four.

25
00:01:36,366 --> 00:01:39,300
You might ask: is the fourth worker slacking?

26
00:01:39,566 --> 00:01:42,900
No. They're constantly moving, always have something in hand.

27
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:46,233
The problem is at the far end of the counter.

28
00:01:46,433 --> 00:01:54,833
There's only one sealing machine. Sealing, labeling, and packing all happen there —
about 40 seconds per cup.

29
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:57,100
That caps out at 90 cups an hour, tops.

30
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:02,300
No matter how fast the prep runs, every cup still has to wait in line for that machine.

31
00:02:02,566 --> 00:02:08,933
The cup the fourth worker just finished doesn't reach the customer — it lands in a queue in front of the machine.

32
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:12,766
So the busyness you see from outside the line is real busyness.

33
00:02:12,966 --> 00:02:14,733
But busy doesn't mean cups coming out.

34
00:02:14,966 --> 00:02:16,333
So here's the question.

35
00:02:16,433 --> 00:02:22,566
Same act of adding one person — why is the second worker worth 30 cups, and the fourth only worth 5?

36
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:30,300
Economics has a name for that extra sliver: marginal return.

37
00:02:30,833 --> 00:02:34,933
In plain terms: how many extra cups did adding this one person produce?

38
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:39,333
Notice — it's the extra amount, not the shop's total output.

39
00:02:39,566 --> 00:02:47,866
That sequence of numbers — 30, 30, 20, 5 — each new hire's added value keeps dropping.

40
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:50,100
That's diminishing marginal returns.

41
00:02:50,366 --> 00:02:55,300
It comes with a condition: the counter is still the same counter, the sealing machine still the same machine.

42
00:02:55,566 --> 00:02:58,166
Only people are being added — nothing else.

43
00:02:58,433 --> 00:03:00,100
This isn't unique to bubble tea shops.

44
00:03:00,366 --> 00:03:04,700
OpenStax's economics textbook has a classic example: logging.

45
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:09,566
The tool is a two-person saw, and workers get added one at a time.

46
00:03:09,566 --> 00:03:11,266
One worker cuts 4 trees.

47
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:16,666
Two workers: 10 trees, up by 6 — because the saw needs two people pulling anyway.

48
00:03:16,966 --> 00:03:19,533
Three workers: 12 trees, up by 2.

49
00:03:19,766 --> 00:03:22,033
Four workers: 13 trees, up by 1.

50
00:03:22,266 --> 00:03:25,366
A fifth worker joins — still 13 trees, up by 0.

51
00:03:25,766 --> 00:03:29,266
The first time I saw this table, I stared at the fifth worker's column for a while.

52
00:03:29,466 --> 00:03:37,700
Then it clicked: there's only one saw. The fifth worker gets to the woods and, aside from hauling a few branches, mostly just stands there watching.

53
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:44,766
Look — total output kept rising, from 4 trees to 13. It never dropped.

54
00:03:44,966 --> 00:03:47,866
What dropped is the slice each new worker adds.

55
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:49,966
This is where a lot of people get confused.

56
00:03:50,633 --> 00:03:55,433
Diminishing doesn't mean the total shrinks — it means each later person is worth less.

57
00:03:55,766 --> 00:04:00,233
Back to the bubble tea shop — that fourth worker's 5 cups are real. They didn't work for nothing.

58
00:04:00,566 --> 00:04:04,766
It's just that most of their effort gets absorbed by that constantly running sealing machine.

59
00:04:05,066 --> 00:04:06,500
So what should the owner do?

60
00:04:07,466 --> 00:04:08,833
One condition first.

61
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:14,166
This math only holds because the owner only added people — the shop and the machine stayed the same.

62
00:04:14,466 --> 00:04:20,100
If he'd also swapped in a sealing machine twice as fast, that's a different calculation — not today's topic.

63
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:25,700
With everything else unchanged, before hiring again, I'd suggest the owner do one thing first.

64
00:04:26,033 --> 00:04:31,300
Follow the new hire who's about to join the counter, and see exactly where they stop and wait.

65
00:04:31,666 --> 00:04:40,733
If they're waiting on a person — say the order counter sits empty while customers stand there ignored —
then adding an order-taker really does add a few cups an hour.

66
00:04:41,066 --> 00:04:50,166
If they're waiting on a machine — say five or six cups are always queued at the sealer —
adding more people just moves the line from the door to behind the counter.

67
00:04:50,433 --> 00:04:53,900
It's the same line. It just moved.

68
00:04:53,966 --> 00:04:56,566
Here's what this shop's owner actually did.

69
00:04:56,633 --> 00:05:02,633
The next day, instead of hiring, he spent over 2,000 yuan on a used sealing machine and put it at the other end of the counter.

70
00:05:02,900 --> 00:05:07,500
Same four workers — output jumped from 85 to over 120 cups an hour.

71
00:05:07,766 --> 00:05:11,166
For the first time, the line outside cleared before 7:30.

72
00:05:11,466 --> 00:05:17,633
So next time you see a chaotic line, you might ask a little less: "how many more people do we need?"

73
00:05:18,433 --> 00:05:21,100
And take one more look: "which machine keeps running nonstop?"

74
00:05:21,566 --> 00:05:29,733
But if tomorrow what changes isn't the staffing but the price on the cup — 15 yuan becomes 18 —
would you still be standing in this line?
